# /rtc_cm/reset_fault — clear a LATCHED controller-local fault (issue #260).
#
# The compliance controllers latch SAFE_STOP on a critical fault (nan_inf,
# pose_error_exceeded, sigma_below_critical, command_divergence) and never
# auto-recover — §10.6 "자동 복구 금지". This service is the only external exit
# from that latch.
#
# Deliberately SEPARATE from the CM global E-STOP (E-8): clearing a
# controller-local fault does NOT clear the global E-STOP latch, and clearing
# the global E-STOP does NOT clear a controller-local fault. Both may be
# latched at once, and each needs its own clear. A reset issued while the
# global E-STOP is latched succeeds on its own terms — the RT loop keeps
# calling Compute() during E-STOP and only substitutes the output — but the
# arm stays held until the global latch is cleared too; `message` says so.
#
# Authority: the request targets the ACTIVE controller only, and
# `controller_name` must name it explicitly. Naming it is the operator
# confirmation step — there is no wildcard and no implicit target, so a fault
# can never be cleared on a controller the caller did not mean to name. A
# request for a non-active controller is refused rather than queued, because a
# pending request on an inactive controller would be consumed on the first tick
# after it is activated, which is exactly the "activation launders a fault"
# path that BeginBiasCalibration() already refuses.

# Controller to reset. REQUIRED, and must equal the currently active
# controller's name (as reported by /rtc_cm/list_controllers).
string controller_name

---

# True iff the latch is confirmed gone — the request was delivered AND the
# controller was observed out of SAFE_STOP one control period later. Also true
# for the no-op case (the named controller had no latched fault).
#
# False means the latch is still up: the fault cause is still present (the
# state machine re-latches on the same tick it is reset), the named controller
# is not the active one, or the name was empty.
bool ok

# Human-readable outcome, including whether the global E-STOP is still latched.
string message
